      "claim": "Because the hospital had lost its protected status, the warning was effective, the target was a legitimate military objective, and the attack was conducted with feasible precautions, the strike on 13 August did not violate the protections owed to civilian medical units under the law of international armed conflict.",
      "reasoning": "The convergence of the loss of protection, compliance with the warning requirement, lawful targeting under the distinction rule, and observance of precautionary measures collectively satisfy the legal tests for a permissible attack. Accordingly, the strike falls within the bounds of the Geneva Conventions, Additional Protocol I, and the Rome Statute, and does not constitute a war crime.",
